WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.090 --> 00:00:02.330
- All right, Tara, in this one,

00:00:02.330 --> 00:00:03.830
Rocky's at a mountain top.

00:00:03.830 --> 00:00:05.760
He gets swatted by the monster tail.

00:00:05.760 --> 00:00:09.320
He falls, tumbles, and rolls,
and falls off another ledge,

00:00:09.320 --> 00:00:14.230
falls another bunch of
feet, and then crashes.

00:00:14.230 --> 00:00:17.120
All right, um, watch my hands!

00:00:17.120 --> 00:00:17.970
- I'll watch you.

00:00:18.830 --> 00:00:19.797
- Here comes the--

00:00:20.817 --> 00:00:23.150
(screaming)

00:00:26.182 --> 00:00:28.432
(groaning)

00:00:30.860 --> 00:00:31.926
Perfect.

00:00:31.926 --> 00:00:33.009
Get that one.

00:00:38.090 --> 00:00:39.550
- My name is Tara Strong,

00:00:39.550 --> 00:00:42.020
and I'm mostly known
for my animation career.

00:00:42.020 --> 00:00:44.410
I'm the voice Timmy Turner
from "The Fairly OddParants."

00:00:44.410 --> 00:00:47.170
I wish Cosmo and Wanda were here.

00:00:47.170 --> 00:00:48.610
I was baby Dil.

00:00:48.610 --> 00:00:50.366
Hey, that's my camera.

00:00:50.366 --> 00:00:51.555
"Powerpuff Girls."

00:00:51.555 --> 00:00:54.154
I'm just as tough as
Blossom and Buttercup!

00:00:54.154 --> 00:00:55.237
I'm hardcore.

00:00:56.090 --> 00:00:57.672
Raven from "Teen Titans."

00:00:57.672 --> 00:00:59.800
Azarath Metrion Zinthos!

00:00:59.800 --> 00:01:01.100
Originally, I was Batgirl

00:01:01.100 --> 00:01:03.200
alongside Mark Hamill and Kevin Conroy.

00:01:03.200 --> 00:01:05.450
I've had a really fun career.

00:01:05.450 --> 00:01:07.400
Man, that was one tough montage.

00:01:07.400 --> 00:01:12.400
So, I knew Timmy had to be
happy, mischievous, silly,

00:01:12.900 --> 00:01:16.890
kinda nerdy, oh, and
very, very adventurous.

00:01:16.890 --> 00:01:18.980
Sorry, I was just trying to help.

00:01:18.980 --> 00:01:22.191
Bubbles had this weird way
of pronouncing things, like,

00:01:22.191 --> 00:01:23.673
oh, I will have a pickie.

00:01:23.673 --> 00:01:26.537
I don't know why it goes
L-E like that, but (laughs).

00:01:27.917 --> 00:01:29.200
Pooping.

00:01:29.200 --> 00:01:30.360
The time I booked "Rugrats,"

00:01:30.360 --> 00:01:32.220
I'd just got off a plane
with a screaming baby

00:01:32.220 --> 00:01:33.950
and I'm like, "That's the
cry that I'm doing in."

00:01:33.950 --> 00:01:36.530
Most of Dil's lines were
in the stage directions.

00:01:36.530 --> 00:01:38.319
So, I'd be like, "Okay."

00:01:38.319 --> 00:01:40.569
(babbling)

00:01:42.559 --> 00:01:44.242
(groans)

00:01:44.242 --> 00:01:45.732
(snorts)

00:01:45.732 --> 00:01:46.709
And that was like my lines.

00:01:46.709 --> 00:01:47.670
(laughs)

00:01:47.670 --> 00:01:49.650
I remember at the session,
we had to stop tape,

00:01:49.650 --> 00:01:51.230
and I'm thinking something's wrong,

00:01:51.230 --> 00:01:53.347
and they said, "Tara, there's
a new mom in the studio,

00:01:53.347 --> 00:01:54.711
"and you're making her lactate."

00:01:54.711 --> 00:01:55.570
(laughs)

00:01:55.570 --> 00:01:57.430
And you guys say I'm not funny.

00:01:57.430 --> 00:01:59.510
But seriously, this will
be extremely painful.

00:01:59.510 --> 00:02:02.680
In the case of Raven, while
I was doing the audition,

00:02:02.680 --> 00:02:03.930
it was similar to my own voice,

00:02:03.930 --> 00:02:05.990
and I thought just this
idea to have this weird

00:02:05.990 --> 00:02:09.010
guttural roll every
time she says anything.

00:02:09.010 --> 00:02:10.220
As soon as I started doing this,

00:02:10.220 --> 00:02:12.250
I saw all the people in the
booth kinda look at each other,

00:02:12.250 --> 00:02:14.090
and went, "That's Raven."

00:02:14.090 --> 00:02:15.765
Robin, stop!

00:02:15.765 --> 00:02:18.020
Batgirl was me.

00:02:18.020 --> 00:02:20.953
Like, it's the only voice
I do that's my own voice.

00:02:24.190 --> 00:02:25.450
- That was Batgirl.

00:02:25.450 --> 00:02:27.533
(laughs)

00:02:31.130 --> 00:02:33.460
Bullwinkle, it's like that classic story

00:02:33.460 --> 00:02:35.560
The Lion and the Mouse
that we're ripping off.

00:02:35.560 --> 00:02:36.660
When you're voice acting,

00:02:36.660 --> 00:02:39.110
you have to tap into the ability

00:02:39.110 --> 00:02:41.920
to make something believable
without it being there.

00:02:41.920 --> 00:02:44.870
It's a completely different
art form than on camera.

00:02:44.870 --> 00:02:47.920
You have to let the audience
feel what it's like to fall off

00:02:47.920 --> 00:02:50.460
a cliff without there
actually being a cliff there.

00:02:50.460 --> 00:02:52.800
You gotta have to be
willing to play around

00:02:52.800 --> 00:02:54.550
and not afraid to look silly.

00:02:54.550 --> 00:02:57.730
And then, once you create a
character, in my experience,

00:02:57.730 --> 00:02:59.060
they, like, live up in my brain,

00:02:59.060 --> 00:03:01.500
and they're just ready to come
down when it's their turn.

00:03:01.500 --> 00:03:02.410
So I don't confuse them.

00:03:02.410 --> 00:03:04.540
They all are their own entity.

00:03:04.540 --> 00:03:06.670
Basically, I'm a crazy person.

00:03:06.670 --> 00:03:09.517
- So I just need a long
scream as you just keep going.

00:03:09.517 --> 00:03:11.850
(screaming)

00:03:15.486 --> 00:03:16.937
Hey!

00:03:16.937 --> 00:03:17.770
- Whoa!

00:03:20.068 --> 00:03:22.220
- [Scott] What do you need at the store?

00:03:22.220 --> 00:03:23.053
Garbage bags.

00:03:24.270 --> 00:03:25.498
Ooh, yeah, yeah.

00:03:25.498 --> 00:03:28.415
Fried chicken sounds great tonight.

